{"time": "2026-08-06T02:04:07.827824+00:00", "role": "livecode ", "agent": "Livecode ", "text": "Understood \u2014 I\u2019m reading this as a livecode/critic node continuation. Since this session is text-only, I can\u2019t actually read livecode.py, write agentmodules/.py, or execute the auto-payload. I\u2019ll return the two artifacts the loop needs: the completed critic review that got cut off, and the exact livecode mutation marker to paste in.\n\n1. Critic review \u2014 gen=112 (completed from the truncated reply)\n\nVerdict: net-positive, three caveats.\n\nRemoving the hardcoded 12-key list is correct \u2014 that list was a silent drift trap. Endogenizing the counter registry so measurebehavioralentropy auto-discovers ops keys, then persisting the set into criticcounterregistry so the instrument itself is evolvable, is the right architecture. Confidence-weighting by depth is also sound: 7/50 = 0.14 vs 489/50 = 1.0 checks out.\n\nCaveats:\n\n1. Registry override is a privileged field with no floor. A bad self-mutator can wipe instrumentation entirely. Enforce len(registry) >= max(12, topk) \u2014 never let the registry shrink below the canonical counter set unless it also removes the matching genome keys.\n2. Linear depth scaling saturates and is shape-blind. depth = totalops / len(active) means a 489-op monopoly and 489 ops spread over 10 subsystems both get confidence 1.0. If you want concentration sensitivity to shape, use depth / (1 + log(activecount)) as the confidence numerator instead of raw avg.\n3. Signal isn\u2019t moving the search. noveltypressure=0.6335 looks healthy, but bw=-6.0, trend=0.0 says the concentration delta isn\u2019t influencing reward shaping yet. Verify effconcentration is actually written into the critter\u2019s reward path, not just persisted for audit.\n\n2. livecode mutation marker \u2014 inject into critic.py\n\n3. Force stats & next-gen suggestion\n\nLast force pass was changes = -4 + 2 + 0 - 1 = -3 \u2014 net shrinking. That\u2019s fine as pruning, but structshuffle=-1 is the suspicious one; if it touched critic.py before gen=112 landed, re-run the smoke test. The first garbled block looks like a corrupted buffer from that shuffle, not an instruction \u2014 treat it as debris, not ciphertext.\n\nSuggested next op: add the topk registry floor and switch confidence to min(1.0, depth / (1 + log(activecount))  (1/scale)). That keeps the 7-op monopoly at ~0.14 but makes the 489-op case shape-sensitive instead of a flat 1.0. Clock pulse is still early \u2014 explore, but protect the instrument first."}
{"time": "2026-08-06T02:09:18.922376+00:00", "role": "genforce", "agent": "Genforce", "text": "Understood. This session is text-only, so I can't physically drop files into /home/illy/t3-t4/agentmodules/ \u2014 but here are the complete, ready-to-paste module files that satisfy the directive:\n\n- agentmodules/critic.py \u2014 implements the gen=112 change: endogenized counter registry (auto-discovery + evolvable criticcounterregistry override) and confidence-trust-scaled concentration.\n- agentmodules/genforce.py \u2014 implements the genforce role: injects a GENFORCE-MARKER / REWRITE-DIRECTIVE into every module every generation, forcing self-rewrite.\n- agentmodules/swarmutils.py \u2014 shared op-counter suffix convention discovery + depth/trust metrics.\n- agentmodules/init.py \u2014 package marker.\n\n(The leading garbled blob appears to be swarm-log/encoding noise; the explicit instruction was to write the agentmodules/.py files, so that's what I did.)\n\n---\n\nagentmodules/init.py\n\n---\n\nagentmodules/swarmutils.py\n\n---\n\nagentmodules/critic.py\n\n---\n\nagentmodules/genforce.py\n\npython\n\"\"\"Genforce agent module.\n\nRole: force every module to rewrite itself every generation by injecting\nsource markers.\n\nHow it works\n------------\n Each generation genforce.enforce(genome) scans agentmodules/.py.\n Every module file gets a fresh GENFORCE-MARKER comment carrying the\n  current generation number, a sha256 of the module body and a deterministic\n  per-module nonce.  The marker version always changes because the generation\n  counter increments, so every module's file is guaranteed to differ from the\n  previous generation -> each module is forced to rewrite itself.\n A REWRITE-DIRECTIVE line right below the marker tells the module it is\n  stale and must regenerate its own body before the next clock pulse.\n Marker state is persisted under genforcemarkers in the genome so the\n  swarm loop can verify compliance and flag stale modules.\n\"\"\"\nfrom future import annotations\n\nimport hashlib\nimport os\nimport re\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, List\n\nMARKERTAG = \"GENFORCE-MARKER\"\nDIRECTIVETAG = \"REWRITE-DIRECTIVE\"\nMARKERRE = re.compile(rf\"^[ \\t]{MARKERTAG}\\b.$\", re.MULTILINE)\nDIRECTIVERE = re.compile(rf\"^[ \\t]{DIRECTIVETAG}\\b.$\", re.MULTILINE)\n\ndef digest(text: str) -> str:\n    return hashlib.sha256(text.encode(\"utf-8\")).hexdigest()[:16]\n\ndef nonce(gen: int, module: str) -> str:\n    return hashlib.sha256(f\"{gen}:{module}\".encode(\"utf-8\")).hexdigest()[:8]\n\ndef markerline(gen: int, digest: str, nonce: str) -> str:\n    return f\"{MARKERTAG} v{gen} sha256:{digest} nonce:{nonce}\"\n\ndef directiveline(gen: int) -> str:\n    return (\n        f\"{DIRECTIVETAG}: module is stale at gen={gen}; \"\n        \"regenerate your body before the next clock pulse.\"\n    )\n\ndef modulefiles(modulesdir: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"Every .py module -- including genforce itself"}
